The `getPickleData` function does not include any checks for the structure or content of the pickled data before deserialization. This could potentially allow an attacker to overflow memory by providing excessively large or complex serialized data, leading to a denial-of-service (DoS) condition.

To mitigate these vulnerabilities, it is essential to validate and sanitize user-input data before deserialization, or to use safer alternatives like `dill` instead of the `pickle` module. Additionally, implementing rate limiting or other security measures can help prevent DoS attacks.
