<analysis>
The encryption function appears to be using an insecure random static IV (Initialization Vector), which makes it easier for attackers to exploit the vulnerability of Cipher Block Chaining (CBC) mode by leveraging a pre-computed set of ciphertexts. 
  
Moreover, AES expects byte input and integer output, but this function does not handle these potential inconsistencies well. Without handling exceptions for incorrectly formatted inputs or outputs, it may lead to crashes and unpredictable behaviour. The code also lacks a return statement for the encryption result which can be misused if there are subsequent operations on the cipher object.
  
For instance, an attacker could use precomputed values of the static vector (first 16 bytes of encrypted output) to reverse-engineer the key used for encryption in future calls to this function with different inputs and thus potentially recover it.
